---
import Logo from '../Logo.astro';

const filter = `{
  "kinds":   [1059],
  "authors": ["a3f1…9c4e"]
}`;

/*
 * The wordmark is split so each letter can land on its own beat. The <h1> is
 * labelled, so assistive tech gets the word rather than a spelling-out of it.
 */
const wordmark = [...'Concord'];
---

{/*
  The opening screen holds the lockup and nothing else — no tagline, no copy,
  no buttons. Everything that used to share it now waits one scroll down, in
  #the-case. Its height subtracts the nav, which is sticky and so occupies flow
  space above — 4rem on md+, plus the ~2.25rem mobile nav row below it on
  smaller screens.
*/}
<section class="relative overflow-hidden">
  <div class="cx-radial cx-radial-center pointer-events-none absolute inset-0">
  </div>
  <div class="cx-grid-bg cx-grid-bg-center pointer-events-none absolute inset-0">
  </div>

  <div
    class="relative mx-auto flex min-h-[calc(100svh-6.25rem)] max-w-4xl flex-col items-center justify-center px-5 py-16 text-center md:min-h-[calc(100svh-4rem)]"
  >
    {/*
      The mark leads and the word labels it, so the mark is set well larger
      than the cap height rather than matching it.

      The two are spaced optically rather than geometrically, which is why the
      mark carries a negative margin instead of the pair carrying a gap: the
      "C" opens toward the word, leaving the right quarter of its viewBox
      blank, and any real gap on top of that void reads as a hole.
    */}
    <h1
      aria-label="Concord"
      class="flex items-center justify-center text-4xl text-white sm:text-6xl lg:text-7xl"
    >
      <Logo
        animate
        class="-me-2.5 h-20 w-20 shrink-0 sm:-me-3 sm:h-28 sm:w-28 lg:-me-3.5 lg:h-32 lg:w-32"
      />
      <span class="cx-wordmark">
        {
          wordmark.map((char, i) => (
            <span class="cx-wordmark-letter" style={`--cx-i:${i}`}>
              {char}
            </span>
          ))
        }
      </span>
    </h1>

  </div>

  {/*
    With nothing else on the screen the cue carries the whole burden of saying
    there is more, so it loses its label — a caption would be the second thing
    on a screen meant to hold one — and gains the small viewports it used to
    skip.
  */}
  <a
    href="#the-case"
    aria-label="Scroll down"
    class="absolute inset-x-0 bottom-6 mx-auto flex w-fit flex-col items-center gap-2 text-gray-500 transition-colors hover:text-mint"
  >
    <span class="cx-scroll-cue text-2xl leading-none" aria-hidden="true"
      >&darr;</span
    >
  </a>
</section>

<!-- The pitch the opening screen used to carry. -->
<section id="the-case" class="relative scroll-mt-20">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-5 py-24 text-center sm:py-28">
    <p
      class="text-balance text-2xl font-medium leading-snug tracking-tight text-gray-200 sm:text-3xl"
    >
      Private communities. <span class="text-mint">Open protocol.</span>
    </p>

    <!--
      Three claims, in priority order: encryption, then the honest relay
      concession carrying the benefit that used to be called decentralization,
      then open-and-ungatekept — evidenced by shipping clients rather than
      asserted.
    -->
    <p class="mx-auto mt-7 max-w-2xl text-base leading-relaxed text-gray-400">
      Every message is sealed on your device before it leaves. Ordinary <a
        href="https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nostr"
        rel="noopener"
        class="text-gray-200 underline decoration-mint/40 underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-mint"
        >Nostr</a
      > relays carry it, but a relay cannot read a message, produce a member list,
      or decide who is in charge. Three independent apps already speak the protocol
      — and nothing is stopping a fourth.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-3">
      <a href="/clients/" class="cx-btn cx-btn-primary">Get a client</a>
      <a href="/spec/" class="cx-btn cx-btn-ghost">Read the specification</a>
      <a
        href="/learn/what-is-concord/"
        class="px-2 py-2 text-sm text-gray-400 transition-colors hover:text-mint"
      >
        How it works
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- The whole client-side view of a community, as a relay query. -->
<section
  id="what-a-relay-sees"
  class="relative border-b border-hairline scroll-mt-20"
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 py-20">
    <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="cx-card p-5 sm:col-span-1">
        <p class="cx-eyebrow">Subscribe</p>
        <pre
          class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-gray-300"><code>{filter}</code></pre>
        <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-gray-500">
          A community is a key. Holding it lets you derive the address; not
          holding it means you cannot even find the room.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cx-card p-5 sm:col-span-2">
        <p class="cx-eyebrow">What comes back</p>
        <p class="cx-cipher mt-3">
          AjMSpVv5aPQ0nUyBu2Zt4Rk9dGx7WqLoE1cHfNiXsY6mTgKrbA8vJlDwOePzZ3hC5uIq
          RtNfXbW1yMoKdSaE7pLgQvHjUcZ0T2nB9rDkAeYxs4iVmJ3wCfOgP6lZtNuHqRbXyKdM
          eSvA1oJpG8cLnWzTiY5rXkQ2fBdHhUuEmNxCsOaZ0tVgIRjPwlbFyD7q4KMcTnAveSXo
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-gray-500">
          Relays, network observers, and non-members see sealed blobs addressed
          to rotating, meaningless labels — never a member list, a channel name,
          or a message.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
